Patch summary:
Run the V4L2 Codec2 HAL on crosvm's virtio-media video decoder.

virtio-media takes MMAP buffers only, so when the device rejects DMABUF buffers
the decoder copies each bitstream buffer into an MMAP input buffer and copies
every decoded picture out of its MMAP output buffer into a YV12 block allocated
like the software decoders allocate theirs, keeping pictures, drains and
resolution changes in decode order. Until the first resolution change only the
EOS buffer is queued, as with DMABUF: crosvm keeps the first capture buffer for
EOS and decodes into any other, at the size it had when it was queued. crosvm
can return pictures of bitstreams that were decoding when a flush came, so
those are recycled rather than passed on as work the flush abandoned. A device
without V4L2 controls (virtio-media) gets the largest H.264 DPB as its minimum
number of output buffers.

When the device offers to decode straight into the output buffers, in the Y
tiles of Intel GPUs (IKYT, a format of Ika's own that its crosvm offers when
the host GPU can), the decoder takes it and un-tiles the visible part of each
picture into the block with libyuv, which spares crosvm copying the picture
into the buffer. In swizzled tiles (IKYS: Haswell and older GPUs with two
memory channels swap rows y and y ^ 4 of every odd 16-byte column), which
libyuv does not take, it un-tiles them itself. Blocks keep the visible size
either way.

The component store lists a decoder only when a /dev/video device takes its
codec, so a VM started without the decoder falls back to the software codecs,
and only once vendor.dlkm.modules.ready says the virtio-media driver has
loaded. A decoder is listed under a ".uhd" name, which media_codecs_c2.xml
gives 4K limits, only when the device decodes pictures up to 4096x4096 and
gives input buffers of at least 4 MB; otherwise it keeps its 1080p name and
limits, and larger video plays on the software decoders. The service joins the
camera group that owns /dev/video*.

